ning a bar. The cops come in all the time for a bite.", "reference": "1993, William S. Burroughs, edited by Oliver Harris, The Letters of William S. Burroughs, 1945–1959, New York: Penguin, →ISBN, page 92:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(television) Ellipsis of sound bite.", "tags": ["abbreviation", "alt of", "countable", "ellipsis", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "cold open: Starting a TV newscast with video or a bite from the lead story rather than starting with the anchor or the standard show open.", "reference": "2015, Robert A. Papper, Broadcast News and Writing Stylebook:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(cricket) The turn that a spin bowler imparts to a pitch.", "tags": ["countable", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bite", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}